diff --git a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
index b5d92dd..6b106c0 100644
--- a/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -73,7 +73,7 @@ The interface uses the Frequency layout: a top bar (the frequency info and the O
 
 Moving between conversations lives in `38-gezinme.js`. Room, direct message and Friends switches add an entry to the browser history, and the address and its `#` part never change (that part can carry a key or an invite). An entry carries only the user id, the distance from the root, the view type and a numeric id (`{ telsiz: { v: 1, u, d, view, id } }`). Sheets, the search panel, settings, the image viewer and the profile card add their own entry at every width (`{ telsiz: { v: 1, u, d, overlay } }`), and the entry is taken back when the layer closes some other way. The back button first closes the open layer, then returns to the previous conversation. When a layer without its own entry (a menu, the emoji picker, the enlarged camera view, a confirmation dialog) is on top, back closes only that layer and moves the history back to where it was, so the conversation does not change. Pressing back on the root entry leaves the app through the browser, no trap entry is added. When the session ends (Log Out or a lost session) the history is rewound to the root entry even when no layer is open. The rewind amount is read from the distance stored in the browser's current entry, so it never goes past the root even when the in-memory model is stale. An entry from another account and a history event outside the app are ignored. Read marks (`telsiz.read.<user>`) are kept only on the device and are not synced between devices. A conversation counts as read only while the person is at the bottom and the newest messages are loaded, so a message that arrives while they are scrolled up is not counted as read. The Mark as Read button reads the whole conversation and asks the server for the id of the newest message when the newest messages are not loaded. When a conversation opens and the first unread message is not on the newest page, the page is requested around the last read message (`around`) and newer messages load as the person scrolls down. The unread count comes from both pages together, and it is a lower bound when the pages do not meet. If the conversation was not counted as read when it opened (the person is not at the bottom), this count and the mentions among those messages go into the counters. The startup scan (`scanUnread`, `scanDmUnread`) skips the conversation that opens at startup or after a reload, so this is what keeps its badge when the person moves to another conversation without scrolling down. This needed no server change. The open view (a text room, a direct conversation or Friends, `telsiz.view.<user>`) is stored on the device and restored when the page reloads.
 
-The last eight opened conversations are cached in memory (`38-gezinme.js`, `06-messages.js restoreChannel`). The cache holds the encrypted envelopes from the server, the paging state (whether older and newer messages exist), the first message in the visible area with its offset, whether the person was at the bottom and the pill count. Decrypted text does not go into the cache, and the messages are decrypted and drawn again on return. On return the list is drawn without the skeleton and without asking the server, and the person comes back to where they were (when they left at the bottom, to the New messages divider if new messages arrived, otherwise to the bottom). Then the newest page is requested in the background and merged: missed new messages are added, messages edited since are refreshed, and messages in the range of the newest page that are missing from it were deleted. When the newest page does not meet the loaded messages, newer messages load as the person scrolls down. Events for cached conversations (new, edited and deleted messages) are applied to the cache too. On a resync (`resync`), when the session starts or ends and when the active group key or the personal identity key changes, the cache is not used. Older messages load on their own when the person gets near the top: when the container of the Load Older Messages button comes within 800 pixels of the visible area (`IntersectionObserver`), or in a browser without the observer when scrolling goes below 400 pixels. They do not load while the person is at the bottom. When the startup scan finds the whole newest page (50 messages) unread and older messages exist, the count is a lower bound and the badge shows "50+". The exact count is written once the conversation is opened and its unread messages are counted. Alt with the up and down arrows moves between conversations, Alt+Shift with the up and down arrows moves to the next unread conversation, and Esc counts the conversation as read. These shortcuts do not work when push to talk or another voice key is bound to Alt, Shift or the arrows. These needed no server change either.
+The last eight opened conversations are cached in memory (`38-gezinme.js`, `06-messages.js restoreChannel`). The cache holds the encrypted envelopes from the server, the paging state (whether older and newer messages exist), the first message in the visible area with its offset, whether the person was at the bottom and the pill count. Decrypted text does not go into the cache, and the messages are decrypted and drawn again on return. On return the list is drawn without the skeleton and without asking the server, and the person comes back to where they were (when they left at the bottom, to the New messages divider if new messages arrived, otherwise to the bottom). Then the newest page is requested in the background and merged: missed new messages are added, messages edited since are refreshed, and messages in the range of the newest page that are missing from it were deleted. When the newest page does not meet the loaded messages, newer messages load as the person scrolls down. Events for cached conversations (new, edited and deleted messages) are applied to the cache too. The rule of the open conversation applies: when the person left at the bottom the oldest messages drop once the list goes over 600 messages, and when they left further up the list is not trimmed and the older pages they loaded stay. On a resync (`resync`), when the session starts or ends and when the active group key or the personal identity key changes, the cache is not used. Older messages load on their own when the person gets near the top: when the container of the Load Older Messages button comes within 800 pixels of the visible area (`IntersectionObserver`), or in a browser without the observer when scrolling goes below 400 pixels. They do not load while the person is at the bottom. When the startup scan finds the whole newest page (50 messages) unread and older messages exist, the count is a lower bound and the badge shows "50+". The exact count is written once the conversation is opened and its unread messages are counted. Alt with the up and down arrows moves between conversations, Alt+Shift with the up and down arrows moves to the next unread conversation, and Esc counts the conversation as read. These shortcuts do not work when push to talk or another voice key is bound to Alt, Shift or the arrows. These needed no server change either.
 
 Data stored on the device lives in the browser's local storage: the session token, the group keyring (`telsiz.keys`), the personal identity key, pinned contact keys, theme and voice settings, the YouTube consent, read marks and the last open view. The search index exists only in the memory of the session and is never written to disk.
 
diff --git a/docs/MIMARI.md b/docs/MIMARI.md
